From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 05:15:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Give a built-in panel the monitor settings its platform
 package names

Hyprland picks a built-in panel's mode and scale well on its own, but it
drives every output at 8 bits per channel and treats every display as
sRGB unless a monitor rule says otherwise, and the one such setting
Omarchy's rules carry is wide-gamut.lua's EDID preset on Apple Silicon.
A platform package can now name a panel that needs more in displays.conf,
as "panel <connector> <width>x<height> <key>=<value> ...", matched like
the notch cutouts: a connector name prefix and the mode. Omarchy reads two
keys, bitdepth (8 or 10) and cm (a Hyprland SDR preset), and skips a key
it doesn't know, so a newer package can add one.

default/hypr/panel-defaults.lua wraps hl.monitor, as wide-gamut.lua does
for cm, so the settings survive every rule for that panel: monitors.lua,
the display panel's saved settings and the commands' hyprctl eval. The
catch-all names no output and would reach external monitors, so the
panel gets a copy of it of its own, with the settings; a panel that
arrives after the config has loaded gets it then. A bitdepth or cm set in
monitors.lua for the port, the monitor's description or every monitor
wins, key by key. Loaded after wide-gamut.lua, the panel's preset takes
the place of wide-gamut's EDID one for that panel only.

Display P3 on a native-P3 laptop panel follows #10549 (j-c-m), which sets
cm = "dp3" for the Dell XPS OLED at install. Here the platform package
names the panel instead, so no user file is edited and the setting
follows package updates.

Omarchy ships no panel line, so without a platform package that names a
panel nothing changes and hl.monitor is left alone. Older readers of
displays.conf skip the directive.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
